Re-register the RFCOMM profiles when BlueZ drops them.

nocturned registers its SPP (channel 2) and iAP2 (channel 1) profiles once at
startup via org.bluez.ProfileManager1.RegisterProfile, then moves the returned
handle into a task that drains its request stream. BlueZ owns the SDP record
and the listening socket only for as long as that handle lives.

When the stream ends -- bluetoothd restarts, the adapter goes away, D-Bus
errors -- the task falls out of its `while let` loop, the handle drops, and
BlueZ tears down both the record and the listener. Nothing logs it and nothing
re-registers, so the daemon keeps running with no serial port at all until the
device is rebooted.

ACL and AVRCP survive that independently, so macOS still reports the Car Thing
as Connected while the Nocturne connector -- which hardcodes channel 2 and
never performs an SDP query -- fails every dial with "could not open RFCOMM
channel 2" and parks the device on "Needs Probe".

Each registration now runs under a supervisor loop that re-registers with
exponential backoff (1s to 30s, reset on success), re-asserts the adapter
properties and pairing agent that a bluetoothd restart also drops, and
broadcasts bluetooth.profile registered/unregistered so the UI can tell
"listening" from "the record is gone". The first registration still returns
Err, so a profile that can never be registered remains a startup failure.

Also corrects the stale-advertisement hint: the device image is BusyBox init
plus supervisord, with no systemd, so `sudo systemctl restart bluetooth` is not
a command anyone can run on it.

scripts/build-nocturned.sh applies this and cross-compiles the result in Docker
in about 90 seconds; the notes below are for doing it by hand.

Apply in a checkout of https://github.com/usenocturne/nocturned:

    cd /path/to/nocturned
    git apply /path/to/claude-thing/patches/nocturned-spp-reregister.patch

